board_modern_avr — every supported modern AVR, in one board
============================================================

Where board_atmega4809 and board_attiny3217 are per-family demos, this board is
the *coverage* board: it proves that **every** modern core this fork supports
boots and runs. That is all 23:

  tinyAVR 1-series (15): attiny212 214 412 414 416 417 814 816 817
                         1614 1616 1617 3214 3216 3217
  megaAVR 0-series (8):  atmega808 809 1608 1609 3208 3209 4808 4809

How it works
------------

  * blink_fw.c is a single device-agnostic firmware that touches only what is
    identical on every modern AVR: PA0 (the one port+pin on every part) and
    USART0 (same base 0x0800 everywhere). It blinks PA0 and emits one byte per
    loop on USART0 — no PORTMUX / pin direction, since the modern USART model
    raises its wire IRQ the instant TXDATAL is written. The Makefile compiles it
    once per device into fw_<device>.axf, passing the device name in MCU_NAME so
    each ELF's .mmcu section names the right core.

  * runner.c (the host) loads each fw_*.axf, instantiates the core named in its
    .mmcu section, runs ~100 ms of simulated time, and checks that PA0 toggled
    AND USART0 transmitted — i.e. the core booted and both peripherals ran. It
    prints a PASS/FAIL line per device (PA0x<n> USART0-TXx<n>) and exits
    non-zero if any core fails.

Build & run
-----------

    make            # builds fw_<device>.axf for every toolchain-supported core
    make run        # runs the host runner over all of them

Expected output (one line per device, then a summary):

    PASS  attiny3217   fw_attiny3217.axf PA0x10 USART0-TXx10
    ...
    23/23 cores PASSED

Toolchain note
--------------

Building firmware needs a modern avr-gcc (12+) with the tinyAVR-1 / megaAVR-0
device headers (verified with avr-gcc 16). The Makefile filters the device list
down to the cores the installed avr-gcc can actually target, so a plain `make`
never fails on an older toolchain — it just builds fewer (or no) firmwares, and
the host runner builds regardless. `make run` then exercises whatever fw_*.axf
are present (the .axf are build artifacts, not committed — see .gitignore).

Adding a new modern device
--------------------------

Drop its sim_<device>.c in simavr/cores/ (per the auto-discovery in
simavr/Makefile), then add its avr-gcc name to ALL_MCUS in this Makefile. No
firmware edits are needed — blink_fw.c is device-agnostic.
